he closed spec's record in the same rewrite.\n  - **Replay.** It trusts the record without re-validating successor state or reading history, joins recorded and drafted successors in one lineage operation, and refuses every unequal or missing request-and-record pair, a malformed record, and an unreadable merged spec.\n  - **The fence grammar.** Every line beginning with a fence marker is a fence line; a disposition fence stands at column zero, opens only as TOML and closes only as a bare paired fence.\n\n  -> what its chain shows: SR-148's new arm (\"names an already-open work item that supersedes the closed work item\") is what this row decomposes. Every Trust ruling on the request, the record, the bound authority and absent inputs has a clause here, and the Done-when's close, sweep and parser arms map onto them. TC-360 drives each through named cases, including review 015's valid-then-unclosed reproduction at the close and the sweep -> why it is ready: the contract is refactor-proof (input grammar, the open-state set, the bound tree, the record's shape and order, each refusal) and fail-closed on every unreadable input. It names no code symbol and has no `shall`. It carries no Hat-Refs, rightly: SR-148's UNATTENDED-OPS lens already says why it exists.\n- [APPROVE] LLR-345 -> the obligation:\n  - A commit that changes an adoption record, or moves a requesting spec into either complete folder, is admitted only as the close's own commit or as a carriage of an existing record with its request unchanged in some parent. A merge is judged only on changes absent from every parent.\n  - The close's own commit is defined exactly: one readable parent; paired moves from one active branch to complete; nothing else created or destroyed; the composed close subject; every changed path outside `docs/work` exactly the inbound relink those moves wrote, per the shared byte-exact relink reading; the record equal to its request; and each adopted id valid in the parent tree.\n  - The tree