\n- [MEANING+BLESS] LLR-161 Detail -> before: the close's report-first discipline, one disposition row per partial or cancelled close, and a continuing successor drafted with lineage -> after: the same, plus: a disposition owing a successor drafts one or adopts an already-filed one that supersedes the disposed row -> not the same: a close that adopts rather than drafts is newly admissible. Ready to bless. It states the discharge in its own voice and leaves validation and recording to LLR-335, so the rule keeps one home, and it adds no symbol name.\n- [MEANING] LLR-153 Detail -> before: the mint invariant, the three triggers, drafts-not-mints and the approval-dial arms -> after: the same, plus: an adopts-only block allocates no id; a standing successor's title identity and lineage resolve together; every replay re-points to the full successor set -> not the same: three new mint obligations. NOT blessed, for one reason. \"Title identity and lineage resolve together\" does not say what happens when they disagree. WI-902's Trust contract, which this row decomposes, is explicit: \"A standing row found by exact title must carry the draft's `supersedes`, or the mint refuses before any write.\" TC-147's Method states that refusal (\"refusing a title match on another lineage before any write\"), but the design row does not. So a refactor that resolved the pair together and then, on a mismatch, re-pointed or minted anyway would keep the row true. A design row must say where it refuses. The other two clauses are sound.\n- [MEANING] TC-147 Expected and Method -> before: the intake suite's trigger, draft, open-item, census, context-block and dial arms, satisfying SR-174 via LLR-153 -> after: the same, plus: an adopts-only block allocates no id, a drafted title resolving identity and lineage together (refusing a match on another lineage before any write, keeping one on its own lineage), and a replay re-pointing a late dependent to the full successor set (drafts and adoptions together, committing nothing when nothing is left) -> not the same: new arms enter the verified set. NOT blessed, for one reason. Expected says replay re-points \"the full successor set required by SR-148\". SR-148 does not state that requirement. Its amended acceptance says only that processing mints a successor once or names an already-open superseding row. The full-set replay is LLR-153's clause, and LLR-335's \"joins recorded and drafted successors in one lineage operation\". A reader following the citation to SR-148 finds nothing to check it against. The Method is sound and every cited case exists.\n- [MEANING] TC-156 Expected and Method -> before: the handback suite's report-first, loud-no-report, refused-second-close and re-sweep arms, satisfying SR-144 via LLR-161 -> after: the same, plus: a disposition owing a successor drafts or adopts, with no new id and \"full-successor replay as SR-148 requires\", and a Method repeating LLR-153's mint arms (adopts-only allocates no id, title identity and lineage together, replay re-points the full set) -> not the same: new arms enter the verified set. NOT blessed, for the same mis-citation of SR-148 as TC-147. One related note for the rework: the Method's mint arms are LLR-153's, which TC-147 already verifies. Here they sit under a TC whose LLR, LLR-161, states none of them. Trim them to the handback arms, which this TC's own adoption cases hold, or cite LLR-153 for them.\n- [MEANING+BLESS] LLR-312 Detail -> before: the consolidation close's preflight and ordered rewrites -> after: the same, plus: a consolidation verdict that adopts a successor refuses before its first write -> not the same: a new refusal. Ready to bless. It is the Trust section's ruling (a consolidation reconciles absorbs against drafts' `supersedes` only), stated as an observable refusal before any write.\n- [MEANING+BLESS] TC-254 Method -> before: the consolidation mint and close arms on real lanes -> after: the same, plus: an adopting consolidation verdict refuses before its first write and leaves the lane tree byte-identical -> not the same: a new arm. Ready to bless. It verifies exactly LLR-312's new refusal through a named, existing case, `test_a_consolidation_verdict_that_adopts_refuses_the_close`.\n\nVERDICT: MEANING rows=7\n\n## first-approval\n\nFour lane-added rows await a first approval: LLR-335, LLR-345, TC-360 and TC-383, all under SR-148. I read them against SR-148's amended acceptance, against WI-902's Trust section, Done-when and sitting 016's FIX (carried in WI-969's spec), against the sibling LLRs and TCs in the chain, the arms map, and the tests. All 46 evidence nodes exist. All four rows are ready.\n\n- [APPROVE] LLR-335 -> the obligation:\n  - **The request.** An adoption is a fenced TOML block naming exactly one distinct, well-formed successor id, and the disposed row is resolved from the title's one event token.\n  - **Validation.** It accepts exactly one strict-UTF-8, parseable spec open in draft, queued or active state whose `supersedes` names the disposed row. It refuses every other state, an unreadable or unparseable spec, a missing token, a malformed fence or request, and an unavailable listing.\n  - **The close.** It validates in the tree that becomes its commit parent and writes the validated ids, in request order, as the closed spec's record in the same rewrite.\n  - **Replay.** It trusts the record without re-validating successor state or reading history, joins recorded and drafted successors in one lineage operation, and refuses every unequal or missing request-and-record pair, a malformed record, and an unreadable merged spec.\n  - **The fence grammar.** Every line beginning with a fence marker is a fence line; a disposition fence stands at column zero, opens only as TOML and closes only as a bare paired fence.\n\n  -> what its chain shows: SR-148's new arm (\"names an already-open work item that supersedes the closed work item\") is what this row decomposes. Every Trust ruling on the request, the record, the bound authority and absent inputs has a clause here, and the Done-when's close, sweep and parser arms map onto them. TC-360 drives each through named cases, including review 015's valid-then-unclosed reproduction at the close and the sweep -> why it is ready: the contract is refactor-proof (input grammar, the open-state set, the bound tree, the record's shape and order, each refusal) and fail-closed on every unreadable input. It names no code symbol and has no `shall`. It carries no Hat-Refs, rightly: SR-148's UNATTENDED-OPS lens already says why it exists.\n- [APPROVE] LLR-345 -> the obligation:\n  - A commit that changes an adoption record, or moves a requesting spec into either complete folder, is admitted only as the close's own commit or as a carriage of an existing record with its request unchanged in some parent. A merge is judged only on changes absent from every parent.\n  - The close's own commit is defined exactly: one readable parent; paired moves from one active branch to complete; nothing else created or destroyed; the composed close subject; every changed path outside `docs/work` exactly the inbound relink those moves wrote, per the shared byte-exact relink reading; the record equal to its request; and each adopted id valid in the parent tree.\n  - The tree under judgement is read strictly. A parent spec that does not parse holds no record, and an unreadable parent is refused by name.\n  - It applies at the lane-commit walk and the staged check, squash handling included.\n\n  -> what its chain shows: this is the Trust section's commit check, rebuilt to carry sitting 016's FIX on the owner's route. The relink clause admits a genuine close whose `spec_move` relinks a file outside `docs/work/`, which the mechanical-close attestor alone would refuse, and the record, request, parent-tree and close-shape validation are kept. TC-383 holds every arm, including the real-close relink passing the rider and the squash hook beside a han